three bold, no extra. So passes. But maybe actual selected range not all because cursor? We used Select all. Yes.\n\nCould use existing Single and type multiple lines without newline. Good.\n\nWhat about selection ending at **start of a line** (e.g., select all but excluding last line?) The \"affected lines computed from start and end\" could apply bold to line containing selectionEnd as well, even if selectionEnd exactly at line start, thus bolding an unselected line. That's a classic off-by-one. Our trailing newline case exactly demonstrates: selection end at after newline = start of empty line; code includes line start, adds `****`. The precondition no newline Single helps avoid this false positive for one-line; multi-line trailing newline triggers. But developer couldn't reproduce because maybe they tested Single only (no trailing newline). Still a legitimate bug? They explicitly say Single no newline, so maybe trailing newline scenario not tested/expected. But users can have trailing newline.\n\nCould be the app's design to include line at end position intentionally, but then \"affected lines computed from start and end of selection range\" means exactly includes both. Hmm.\n\nLet's search memory of Markor issue. In Markor, long-clicking a formatting action triggers \"repeat\" or \"alternate action\". For bold, long-click might \"toggle bold for each selected line\". If selection includes trailing newline, it may intentionally bold empty line. But would upstream ship `****`? Could be accepted weirdness. The task likely generated from real regression list; each changed bullet corresponds to a bug introduced. The specific wording \"affected lines are now computed from the start and end of the selection range\" suggests change intended to fix lines selection but likely has bug with `selectionEnd` offset. The expected may be to bold only lines **partially covered**? Let's derive correct algorithm from source.\n\nIn Markor, to format selected lines with prefix (e.g.